{"name":"Green Clay","hex":"#889988","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"},{"term":"clay","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A mineral substance made up of small crystals of silica and alumina, that is ductile when moist; the material of pre-fired ceramics.","An earth material with ductile qualities."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To add clay to or to spread clay onto.","To purify using clay."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/clay"}],"entries":[{"title":"Green Clay","blocks":[{"text":"Green Clay was an American businessman, planter, military officer and politician in Virginia and Kentucky. Clay served in the American Revolutionary War and helped form the new state of Kentucky after representing its Madison County in the Virginia Ratifying Convention of 1788 and in the Virginia House of Delegates which ultimately authorized creation of the new state of Kentucky. He also served in Kentucky's constitutional convention and in both houses of the Kentucky General Assembly, which would name a county for him in 1807. During the War of 1812 Clay was commissioned as a general and led the Kentucky militia in the relief of Fort Meigs in Ohio. He was believed to be one of the wealthiest men of the state, owning tens of thousands of acres of land, many slaves, several distilleries, a tavern, and ferries, although one of his sons, Cassius Marcellus Clay would become a prominent abolitionist."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/41/General_Green_Clay.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green_Clay","wikidata":"Q5602421"},{"title":"Clay court","blocks":[{"text":"A clay court is one of the types of tennis court on which the sport of tennis is played. Clay courts are built on a foundation of crushed stone, particularly limestone, shale, and other aggregate, with a thin layer of fine clay particles on top. Clay courts are more common in Continental Europe and Latin America than in North America, Asia-Pacific, or Britain. The only Grand Slam tournament that uses clay courts is the French Open."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/ND_DN_2006FO.jpg/330px-ND_DN_2006FO.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clay_court","wikidata":"Q198718"},{"title":"Clay","blocks":[{"text":"Clay is a type of fine-grained natural soil material containing clay minerals. Most pure clay minerals are white or light-coloured, but natural clays show a variety of colours from impurities, such as a reddish or brownish colour from small amounts of iron oxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Boulder_clay_cliff_south_of_Withernsea_%28geograph_6054962%29.jpg/330px-Boulder_clay_cliff_south_of_Withernsea_%28geograph_6054962%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clay","wikidata":"Q42302"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Munitions_Production_on_the_Home_Front%2C_1914-1918_Q30557.jpg/500px-Munitions_Production_on_the_Home_Front%2C_1914-1918_Q30557.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Munitions_Production_on_the_Home_Front%2C_1914-1918_Q30557.jpg/1280px-Munitions_Production_on_the_Home_Front%2C_1914-1918_Q30557.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Munitions_Production_on_the_Home_Front%2C_1914-1918_Q30557.jpg/500px-Munitions_Production_on_the_Home_Front%2C_1914-1918_Q30557.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A clay court being rolled at Gretna Green, Scotland, in the 1910s","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMunitions_Production_on_the_Home_Front%2C_1914-1918_Q30557.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Tennis_Courts%2C_Kirkton_Park_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1306458.jpg/500px-Tennis_Courts%2C_Kirkton_Park_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1306458.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Tennis_Courts%2C_Kirkton_Park_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1306458.jpg/500px-Tennis_Courts%2C_Kirkton_Park_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1306458.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A shale or \"blaes\" court, as seen here in Scotland","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATennis_Courts%2C_Kirkton_Park_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1306458.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Sanchai_and_Sonchat_Ratiwatana_Davis_Cup_vs_PH.jpg/500px-Sanchai_and_Sonchat_Ratiwatana_Davis_Cup_vs_PH.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Sanchai_and_Sonchat_Ratiwatana_Davis_Cup_vs_PH.jpg/1280px-Sanchai_and_Sonchat_Ratiwatana_Davis_Cup_vs_PH.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Sanchai_and_Sonchat_Ratiwatana_Davis_Cup_vs_PH.jpg/500px-Sanchai_and_Sonchat_Ratiwatana_Davis_Cup_vs_PH.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Thai players Sanchai and Sonchat Ratiwatana on a shell court in the Philippines","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASanchai_and_Sonchat_Ratiwatana_Davis_Cup_vs_PH.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Maria_Sharapova%2C_2008_Family_Circle_Cup.JPG/500px-Maria_Sharapova%2C_2008_Family_Circle_Cup.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Maria_Sharapova%2C_2008_Family_Circle_Cup.JPG/1280px-Maria_Sharapova%2C_2008_Family_Circle_Cup.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Maria_Sharapova%2C_2008_Family_Circle_Cup.JPG/500px-Maria_Sharapova%2C_2008_Family_Circle_Cup.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Maria 